The `writeCorbellToTypedStore` verification reads (lines 231-232) call `countTypedNodes(storePath)` and `countTypedEdges(storePath)`, each of which opens a brand-new connection via `openTypedStore`. That function runs `PRAGMA journal_mode = WAL`, `PRAGMA busy_timeout = 5000`, `applyTypedDdl(db)` (which creates tables, indexes, and deferred-index recovery), plus post-open identity verification — all just to execute a single `SELECT COUNT(*)`. This is very heavy overhead for a verification count.

Since the writer connection (`db`) is still open and has just committed, a simpler and cheaper approach would be to run the count queries directly on `db`:
```ts
const nRow = db.query("SELECT COUNT(*) AS n FROM typed_nodes WHERE superseded_run IS NULL").get() as { n: number };
const eRow = db.query("SELECT COUNT(*) AS n FROM typed_edges WHERE superseded_run IS NULL").get() as { n: number };
storeNodes = Number(nRow.n);
storeEdges = Number(eRow.n);
```
This avoids opening two additional connections and running full DDL on each.

